## 38.9 Descriptors

### 英文原文

> Very briefly previewed in Chapter 32, descriptors provide an alternative way to intercept attribute access; they are strongly related to the properties discussed in the prior section.
>
> Really, a property is a kind of descriptor—technically speaking, the property built-in is just a simplified way to create a specific type of descriptor that runs method functions on attribute accesses.
>
> In fact, descriptors are the underlying implementation mechanism for a variety of class tools, including both properties and slots, and play other internal roles in Python that we can safely skip here.
>
> Functionally speaking, the descriptor protocol allows us to route a specific attribute’s get, set, and delete operations to methods of a separate class’s instance object that we provide.
>
> This allows us to insert code to be run automatically on attribute fetches and assignments, intercept attribute deletions, and provide documentation for the attributes if desired.
>
> Descriptors are created as independent classes, and they are assigned to class attributes just like method functions. Like any other class attribute, they are inherited by subclasses and instances.
>
> Their access-interception methods are provided with both a self for the descriptor instance itself as well as the instance of the client class whose attribute references the descriptor object.
>
> Because of this, they can retain and use state information of their own, as well as state information of the subject instance. For example, a descriptor may call methods available in the client class, as well as descriptor-specific methods it defines.
>
> Like a property, a descriptor manages a single, specific attribute; although it can’t catch all attribute accesses generically, it provides control over both fetch and assignment accesses and allows us to change an attribute name freely from simple data to a computation without breaking existing code.
>
> If this sounds like properties, it’s because it is: as you shall see, properties can be coded as descriptors directly.
>
> Unlike properties, though, descriptors provide a more general tool. For instance, because they are coded as normal classes, descriptors have their own state, may participate in descriptor inheritance hierarchies, can use composition to aggregate objects, and provide a natural structure for coding internal methods and attribute documentation strings.

### 中文翻译

> 描述符提供了另一种托管属性的方式。它们功能上很像 property，但更底层、更通用——实际上 property 是基于描述符协议实现的便利层；slots 等机制同样建立在描述符之上。
>
> 简言之，描述符是实现了 `__get__` / `__set__` / `__delete__` 中部分方法的对象；当你把这样的对象赋给**类属性**时，对该名的实例访问会转交给描述符方法。
>
> 与 property 一样，描述符通常管理特定属性名，并能在读写删除时插入代码。差异在于：你是在独立类中显式编码协议，而不是调用 `property` 内建。

### 深度理解

- **核心概念**：实现 `__get__`/`__set__`/`__delete__` 的对象即描述符；**数据描述符**（有 `__set__`/`__delete__`）优先于实例字典。
- 函数、`property`、`slots`、绑定方法都建立在描述符之上。
- 状态可放在描述符实例上（跨客户端共享）或每个宿主实例上（常用 `_name` 或 key in `instance.__dict__`）。
- 自写描述符时切勿 `instance.attr = ...` 再触发自己；用 `instance.__dict__[...]` 或 `object.__setattr__`。
- 读懂描述符 = 读懂 Python 面向对象的底层属性模型。

