## 38.12 Computed Attributes（Descriptors）（计算属性 · Descriptors（描述符））

### 英文原文

> As was the case when using properties, our first descriptor example of the prior section didn’t do much—it simply printed trace messages for attribute accesses as a demo. In practice, descriptors can also be used to compute attribute values each time they are fetched.
>
> Example 38-5 illustrates—it’s a rehash of the same example we coded for properties but uses a descriptor to automatically square an attribute’s value each time it is fetched.
>
>
> ```python
> Example 38-5.
> ```
>
> desc-computed.py ```python
>
>
> ```python
> class DescSquare:
> def __init__(self, start):
> ```
>
> ``` # Each desc has own state ```python self.value = start
>
>
> ```python
> def __get__(self, instance, owner):
> ```
>
> ``` # On attr fetch ```python return self.value ** 2
>
>
> ```python
> def __set__(self, instance, value):
> ```
>
> ``` # On attr assign ```python self.value = value ``` # No delete or docs ```python
>
>
> ```python
> class Client1:
> X = DescSquare(3)
> ```
>
> ``` # Assign descriptor instance to class attr ```python
>
>
> ```python
> class Client2:
> X = DescSquare(32)
> ```
>
> ``` # Another instance in another client class # Could also code two instances in same class ```python c1 = Client1() c2 = Client2() print(c1.X) ``` # 3 ** 2 ```python c1.X = 4 print(c1.X) ``` # 4 ** 2 ```python print(c2.X) ``` # 32 ** 2 (1024) When run, the output of this example is the same as that of the original propertybased version, but here a descriptor class object is intercepting the attribute accesses instead of a property: ```python $ python3 desc-computed.py 9 16 1024 ```

